  <ferc:GeneralInformationChangeInAuditorFlag id="f-16" contextRef="c-1">false</ferc:GeneralInformationChangeInAuditorFlag>
  <ferc:ControlOverRespondentTextBlock id="f-17" contextRef="c-1">&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;NextEra Energy Transmission MidAtlantic, INC. is a direct wholly owned subsidiary of NextEra Energy Transmission MidAtlantic Holdings, LLC, which in turn is a wholly owned subsidiary of NextEra Energy Transmission, LLC (NEET). NEET is a wholly owned subsidiary of NextEra Energy Infrastructure, LLC, which in turn is a wholly owned subsidiary of NextEra Energy Capital Holdings, Inc. (NEECH), a wholly owned subsidiary of NextEra Energy, Inc. (NextEra Energy), a company listed on the New York Stock Exchange.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;</ferc:ControlOverRespondentTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfChangesInAndImportantAdditionsToFranchiseRightsTextBlock id="f-18" contextRef="c-1">&lt;div style="padding-left:9pt;text-indent:-9pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;1.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%;padding-left:1.5pt"&gt;&#160;Not Applicable&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfChangesInAndImportantAdditionsToFranchiseRightsTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfAcquisitionOfOwnershipInOtherCompaniesByReorganizationMergerOrConsolidationWithOtherCompaniesTextBlock id="f-19" contextRef="c-1">&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;2. Not Applicable&lt;/span&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfAcquisitionOfOwnershipInOtherCompaniesByReorganizationMergerOrConsolidationWithOtherCompaniesTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfPurchaseOrSaleOfAnOperatingUnitOrSystemTextBlock id="f-20" contextRef="c-1">&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;3. Not Applicable&lt;/span&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfPurchaseOrSaleOfAnOperatingUnitOrSystemTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfImportantLeaseholdsThatHaveBeenAcquiredOrGivenAssignedOrSurrenderedTextBlock id="f-21" contextRef="c-1">&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;4. Not Applicable&lt;/span&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfImportantLeaseholdsThatHaveBeenAcquiredOrGivenAssignedOrSurrenderedTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfImportantExtensionOrReductionOfTransmissionOrDistributionSystemTextBlock id="f-22" contextRef="c-1">&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;5. Not Applicable&lt;/span&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfImportantExtensionOrReductionOfTransmissionOrDistributionSystemTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfObligationsIncurredOrAssumedByRespondentAsGuarantorForThePerformanceByAnotherOfAnyAgreementOrObligationTextBlock id="f-23" contextRef="c-1">&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;6. In Docket No. ES24-27-000, FERC authorized NEET MA to issue long-term debt securities in an aggregate principal amount not to exceed $250 million. On April 29, 2025, NEET MA entered into a new revolving credit agreement with various lenders, with total commitments of $150 million. Borrowings under the facility bear interest at a rate equal to one-month SOFR, as published by the Federal Reserve Bank of New York, plus a fixed spread of 1.25%. As of December 31, 2025, outstanding borrowings under the revolver totaled $44 million.&lt;/span&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfObligationsIncurredOrAssumedByRespondentAsGuarantorForThePerformanceByAnotherOfAnyAgreementOrObligationTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfChangesInArticlesOfIncorporationOrAmendmentsToCharterTextBlock id="f-24" contextRef="c-1">&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;7. Not Applicable&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfChangesInArticlesOfIncorporationOrAmendmentsToCharterTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfEstimatedAnnualEffectAndNatureOfAnyImportantWageScaleChangesTextBlock id="f-25" contextRef="c-1">&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;8. Not Applicable&lt;/span&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfEstimatedAnnualEffectAndNatureOfAnyImportantWageScaleChangesTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfStatusOfAnyMateriallyImportantLegalProceedingsTextBlock id="f-26" contextRef="c-1">&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;9. On June 12, 2024, NEET MA filed proposed revisions to increase the base return on equity (ROE) in its formula rate, Attachment H-33B to the PJM Tariff, from 9.6% to 11.0%. On May 28, 2025, NEET MA filed a Settlement Agreement providing for a base ROE of 10.27% or 10.77% when combined with its previously authorized RTO Participation Adder. The Settlement also included provisions addressing NEET MA&#8217;s capital structure and the accrual rate that will apply to the extent that NEET MA accrues AFUDC on a construction project. On September 3, 2025, FERC approved the Settlement. NEET MA filed a compliance filing to implement the settlement ROE on October 3, 2025. The compliance filing remains pending before FERC. (ER24-2255)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfStatusOfAnyMateriallyImportantLegalProceedingsTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfMateriallyImportantTransactionsOfTheRespondentNotDisclosedElsewhereTextBlock id="f-27" contextRef="c-1">&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;10. Not Applicable&lt;/span&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfMateriallyImportantTransactionsOfTheRespondentNotDisclosedElsewhereTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfImportantChangesRelatedToRespondentCompanyAppearingInAnnualReportToStockholdersTextBlock id="f-28" contextRef="c-1">&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;12. Not Applicable&lt;/span&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfImportantChangesRelatedToRespondentCompanyAppearingInAnnualReportToStockholdersTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfChangesInOfficersDirectorsMajorSecurityHoldersAndVotingPowersOfTheRespondentTextBlock id="f-29" contextRef="c-1">&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;13. Changes in officers during the period:&lt;br/&gt;09/29/2025 - Matt Pawlowski assigned Assistant Vice President&lt;br/&gt;03/17/2025 - Evan Yager assigned President&lt;br/&gt;03/07/2025 - Matthew R. Geoffroy assigned Assistant Treasurer&lt;br/&gt;03/17/2025 - Andres Perez resigned as President&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfChangesInOfficersDirectorsMajorSecurityHoldersAndVotingPowersOfTheRespondentTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfSignificantEventThatRespondentParticipatesInACashManagementProgramsTextBlock id="f-30" contextRef="c-1">&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;14. Not Applicable&lt;/span&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfSignificantEventThatRespondentParticipatesInACashManagementProgramsTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfImportantDisclosuresRegardingTheFinancialStatementsTextBlock id="f-31" contextRef="c-1">&lt;div style="margin-top:0.75pt;text-align:center"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;NEXTERA ENERGY TRANSMISSION MIDATLANTIC, INC.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:center"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:center"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;NOTES TO FINANCIAL STATEMENTS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:center"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;FOR THE YEARS ENDED DECEMBER 31, 2025 AND 2024&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:center"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;(in thousands, unless otherwise noted)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Introduction&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:0.3pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:0.75pt;text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;The accompanying financial statements were prepared in accordance with the accounting requirements of the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission (FERC) as set forth in its applicable Uniform System of Accounts and published accounting releases, which is a comprehensive basis of accounting that varies from generally accepted accounting principles in the United States of America (GAAP).These requirements differ from GAAP related (1) the presentation of deferred income taxes, (2) the presentation of regulatory debits and credits, (3) the presentation of regulatory assets and liabilities, (4) treatment of equity carrying charges, (5) amortization of goodwill, (6) the presentation of accruals associated with the cost of removal included within the accumulated depreciation reserve, (7) the presentation of the provision of rate refund and accrued interest, and (8) the presentation of debt issuance costs. Accordingly, certain footnotes are not prepared on the same basis as the financial statements presented herein.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:0.75pt;text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:0.75pt;text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;FERC ONLY &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;LEASE DISCLOSURE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;As provided in Docket No. AI19-1-000, the Company has recorded operating leases that may be capitalized in the FERC balance sheet accounts that have already been established for capital lease assets and liabilities (Accounts 101.1, 111, 227, and 243). &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;As of December 31, 2025 the Company&#8217;s right of use assets and lease liabilities for operating leases were included within the following FERC accounts (in thousands):&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:center"&gt;&lt;table style="border-collapse:collapse;display:inline-table;margin-bottom:5pt;vertical-align:text-bottom;width:40.000%"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:48.900%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:48.900%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;Account&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;2025&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;border-top:1pt solid #000;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;101&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;border-top:1pt solid #000;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;border-top:1pt solid #000;padding:2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;145&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;border-top:1pt solid #000;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:center"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;111&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;&#8212;&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;227&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;(97)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:center"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;243&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;(47)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Rent expense related to these lease arrangements will continue to be included within cost-of-service rates. The Company has adhered to the FERC requirements as provided in Docket No. AI19-1-000 and is maintaining unique sub-accounts to separately identify and track the capitalized operating lease amounts and has in place effective internal controls to ensure there is no impact to the existing ratemaking treatment or practices for leases.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:10pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;NOTE 1.&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;ORGANIZATION AND BUSINESS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;NextEra Energy Transmission MidAtlantic, Inc. (the "Company") was initially formed in 2015 as NextEra Energy Transmission MidAtlantic, LLC, a Delaware limited liability company for the purpose of acquiring, developing, owning, and operating transmission facilities in the region of the PJM Interconnection, LLC ("PJM"). In 2018, the Company was domesticated in Illinois. In December 2019, the Company was incorporated in Indiana. In December 2023, the Company name changed from NextEra Energy Transmission MidAtlantic Indiana, Inc. to NextEra Energy Transmission MidAtlantic, Inc. The Company is a direct wholly owned subsidiary of NextEra Energy Transmission MidAtlantic Holdings, LLC, which in turn is a wholly owned subsidiary of NextEra Energy Transmission, LLC ("NEET"). NEET is a wholly owned subsidiary &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;of NextEra Energy Infrastructure, LLC which in turn is a wholly owned subsidiary of NextEra Energy Capital Holdings,&#160;Inc. ("NEECH"), a wholly owned subsidiary of NextEra Energy,&#160;Inc. ("NextEra Energy"), a company listed on the New York Stock Exchange.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:10pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;In October 2020, the Company acquired 20 miles of 345 kV electric transmission lines and related power line poles located in Lake and Porter Counties, Indiana ("Transmission Assets") from Commonwealth Edison Company of Indiana, Inc. ("ComEd IN"). The Transmission Assets constitute the first standalone transmission assets owned by the Company and enable the Company to become a Transmission Owner ("TO") in PJM under the PJM Open Access Transmission Tariff ("PJM Tariff"). ComEd IN will continue to operate and maintain the Transmission Assets on behalf of the Company through the use of its parent Company, Commonwealth Edison Company ("ComEd"), pursuant to an operations and maintenance agreement ("O&amp;amp;M Agreement").&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:10pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;NOTE 2.&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;SIGNIFICANT ACCOUNTING POLICIES&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Regulatory Accounting -&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;The Company is subject to rate regulation by the FERC. The Company&#8217;s rates are designed to recover the cost of providing electric service to its customers and currently include a return on invested capital. Revenues from the Company's cost based formula rates are recorded when earned. The Company achieves matching of costs and related revenues by deferring the net under recovery or over recovery. Any under recovered costs or over recovered revenues are collected from or returned to customers in subsequent periods.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;As a regulated utility, the Company records assets and liabilities that result from the regulated ratemaking process that would otherwise not be recorded under GAAP for non-regulated companies. Regulatory assets represent costs that will be included as a component of future tariff rates and regulatory liabilities represent amounts that have been collected in current rates to recover costs that are expected to be incurred, or refunded to customers, in future periods.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;When regulatory assets are probable of recovery through regulated rates, the Company records them as assets on the balance sheet. Once a regulatory asset is recorded, the Company reviews the probability of recovery at each balance sheet date, considering the impact of any changes or events during the period. If the likelihood of future recovery of any regulatory asset becomes less than probable, the Company will write off that regulatory asset as a charge against income.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:13.5pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:-13.5pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;If the Company were no longer subject to cost-based rate regulation, the existing regulatory assets would be written off. In addition, the regulator has the authority to disallow recovery of costs that it considers excessive or imprudently incurred. The continued applicability of regulatory accounting is assessed at each reporting date.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;See Note 3 - Regulatory Matters for additional information.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Cash and Cash Equivalents&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; - All highly liquid investments with a maturity at the date of purchase of three months or less are considered cash. Cash for short-term working capital needs is held in financial institutions.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Accounts Receivable &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;- Receivables are associated with revenues earned from contracts with customers consisting of both billed and unbilled amounts. Receivables represent unconditional rights to consideration and reflect the differences in timing of revenue recognition and cash collections. If any of the customer receivable balances should be deemed uncollectible, it could have a material adverse effect on the Company's results of operations and financial condition. However, management does not believe significant credit risk existed at December 31, 2025 and 2024. Accounts receivable balances would be adjusted for any write-offs and any estimated allowance for doubtful accounts, which is calculated on the basis of the expected credit losses. The allowance for doubtful accounts is reviewed periodically, considering historical and current information such as average write-offs to estimate the expected credit losses. There was no allowance for doubtful accounts recorded as of December 31, 2025 or 2024, respectively.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Use of Estimates&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; - The preparation of financial statements requires management to make estimates and assumptions that affect the reported amounts of assets and liabilities and disclosure of contingent assets and liabilities at the date of the financial statements and the reported amounts of revenues and expenses during the reporting period. Actual results could differ from those estimates.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Property, Plant and Equipment, Net&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; - Property, plant and equipment, net consists primarily of transmission assets. Property, plant and equipment are recorded at cost and depreciated on a straight-line average remaining life basis. Accumulated depreciation consists of the cost of units of property retired less estimated net salvage value. Maintenance and repairs of property as well as replacements and renewals of items determined to be less than units of property are charged to operations and maintenance expenses. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Construction work in progress includes construction materials, progress payments on major equipment contracts, third-party engineering costs and other costs directly associated with the construction of various projects. Upon completion of the projects, these costs are transferred to property, plant and equipment in service. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Depreciation of the Company's transmission assets is provided on a straight-line basis, primarily over its average remaining useful life as approved by the FERC.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;See Note 4 - Property, plant and equipment, net for additional information.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;AFUDC&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; - Allowance for funds used during construction ("AFUDC") represents the estimated costs of borrowed and equity funds used to finance regulated plant additions before they go into service and is capitalized as part of the cost of construction. AFUDC is recoverable through rates over the life of the related asset once that asset is placed in service. AFUDC related to the cost of borrowed funds is recorded as a reduction to interest expense. AFUDC related to the cost of equity funds is recorded as a component of other income. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;As a component of construction work in progress, AFUDC will be placed into service along with other construction costs attributable to assets placed in service and depreciated over the useful lives of those assets in accordance with rates approved by FERC.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Impairment of Long-Lived Assets&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; - Long-lived assets that are held and used are reviewed for impairment whenever events or changes in circumstances indicate carrying values may not be recoverable. As of December 31, 2025 and 2024, the Company concluded no impairment adjustments were necessary.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Revenue Recognition&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; - Operating revenues are derived primarily from transmission services. Revenues are determined using approved tariff rates and are recognized as transmission service is provided. The tariff rates permit the Company to recover allowable costs and earn a return on investments in property, plant and equipment. The obligation to provide transmission services is satisfied over time as the customer simultaneously receives and consumes benefits. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Income Taxes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; - Deferred income taxes are provided on all significant temporary differences between the financial statement and tax basis of assets or liabilities. The significant temporary differences result primarily from property basis differences caused by differences in book and tax depreciation.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Included in regulatory assets on the Company's balance sheets is the revenue equivalent of the difference in accumulated deferred income taxes computed under accounting rules, as compared to regulatory accounting rules. The resulting excess deferred taxes will be returned to rate payers in the future. The Company is a member of a consolidated group, NextEra Energy and subsidiaries, which files a consolidated tax return. The income tax accounts in the accompanying financial statements are determined as if the Company filed a separate tax return. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;The Company files a separate Indiana income tax return. For FERC regulatory reporting purposes, the income tax provision reflects the use of the &#8220;separate return method&#8221; except that tax benefits that could not be used on a separate return basis, but are used on the consolidated tax return, are recorded by the subsidiary that generated the tax benefits.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:10pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;See Note 7 - Income Taxes for additional information.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:10pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;NOTE 3.&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;REGULATORY MATTERS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Formula Rate Filings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On November 22, 2023, the Company filed a request with FERC for certain transmission rate incentives with respect to its competitively-awarded Mid-Atlantic Resiliency Link transmission project. The incentives requested included authorization to recover 100% of abandoned plant costs if the project is canceled for reasons outside of the Company's control and authorization to include 100% of prudently incurred Construction Work In Progress in rate base for the project. FERC granted the request on January 19, 2024. Since CWIP in rate base during the construction period is considered an acceleration of a return on the allowance for funds used during construction, the Company believes that prior to the Project going into service, CWIP in rate base is recorded as an Alternative Revenue Program (&#8220;ARP&#8221;), with any over or under recovery reflected as a regulatory asset or liability. The income statement offset is reflected within revenue from regulatory mechanisms.As of December 31, 2025, the Company has recognized $3,309 related to this program.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On March 13, 2024, the Company filed a Notice of Succession to notify the FERC that it changed its name from NextEra Energy Transmission MidAtlantic Indiana, Inc. to NextEra Energy Transmission MidAtlantic, Inc. The filing also made changes to the Company&#8217;s formula rate template and implementation protocols to reflect its new name. The FERC accepted the Company&#8217;s Notice of Succession and revised tariff records in a delegated letter order issued April 12, 2024.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On March 27, 2024, the Company filed an application for authorization under section 204 of the Federal Power Act to request authorization to make available long-term, secured debt securities in a principal amount not to exceed $250,000 in order to finance ongoing capital needs associated with construction of the Mid-Atlantic Resiliency Link transmission project, in addition to other construction upgrades, and the ownership and operation of the transmission assets. The FERC accepted the Company&#8217;s request in an order issued May 10, 2024.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On June 12, 2024, the Company filed proposed revisions to increase the base return on equity (ROE) in its formula rate, Attachment H-33B to the PJM Tariff, from 9.6% to 11.0%. On May 28, 2025, the Company filed a Settlement Agreement providing for a base ROE of 10.27% or 10.77% when combined with its previously authorized RTO Participation Adder. The Settlement also included provisions addressing NEET MA&#8217;s capital structure and the accrual rate that will apply to the extent that NEET MA accrues AFUDC on a construction project. On September 3, 2025, FERC approved the Settlement. NEET MA filed a compliance filing to implement the settlement ROE on October 3, 2025. FERC accepted the compliance filing on December 17, 2025 (ER24-2255). Accordingly, we recorded a provision for rate refund of $193,728.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On July 22, 2025, NEET MA filed edits to its formula rate in Attachment H-33 and H-33A to the PJM Tariff to remove legacy language that implied that the entirety of NEET MA's ATRR should be allocated to the ComEd zone. NEET MA submitted an amendment to its filing on September 12, 2025. The purpose of the edits is to eliminate any conflict between Attachment H-33 and the cost allocation provisions in Schedule 12 Appendix A, section 32 of the PJM Tariff. FERC accepted the filing on November 7, 2025 (ER25-2941).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Regulatory Assets and Liabilities&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;The Company&#8217;s regulatory assets and regulatory liabilities are as follows as of December&#160;31:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;table style="border-collapse:collapse;display:inline-table;margin-bottom:5pt;vertical-align:text-bottom;width:100.000%"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:58.344%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:18.900%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.355%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:18.901%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;2025&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;2024&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 19pt;text-align:left;text-indent:-18pt;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:100%;text-decoration:underline"&gt;Regulatory Assets&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;border-top:1pt solid #000;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;border-top:1pt solid #000;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:18pt;text-indent:-18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;Pre-commercial costs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:6.5pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%;position:relative;top:-3.5pt;vertical-align:baseline"&gt;(a)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;12,496&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;29,702&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;Transaction cost regulatory asset&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:6.5pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%;position:relative;top:-3.5pt;vertical-align:baseline"&gt;(b)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;&#8212;&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;3,326&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;Deferred taxes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;892&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;876&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;2023 under recovery&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;&#8212;&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;141&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;2024 under recovery&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;4,747&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;9,930&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;2025 under recovery&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;1,21